\section{Introduction}

Discourse markers (DMs) are linguistic elements that explicitly signal the logical connections between sentences, such as {\it by contrast}, {\it for instance}, and {\it consequently}. These markers are crucial for understanding the logical structure of text. For example, the marker {\it by contrast} indicates that the following sentence presents information opposing the preceding one. Consequently, DMs are vital for various NLP tasks, including machine translation, summarization, and sentiment analysis. Despite their importance, existing models often struggle to effectively capture the logical relations conveyed by DMs, resulting in limited performance on tasks like sentence pair classification. To overcome this limitation, we propose a new method that utilizes a pre-trained language model to learn a representation of the logical relation associated with a DM. This representation is subsequently incorporated into a sentence pair classification model. We refer to this approach as {\it Discourse Marker Embedding} (DME). Our method is evaluated on three sentence pair classification datasets: {\it MultiNLI} \citep{conneau2017supervised}, {\it XNLI} \citep{conneau2018x}, and {\it SemEval-2017 Task 1} \citep{barbosa2017semeval}. The experimental results show that DME surpasses existing state-of-the-art methods on all three datasets.
